  ],
  "description": "Analog circuit reconstruction of one GML 8200 Series II channel. Massenburg's published 1972 inverted tracking-notch method places each gain control ahead of the resonator and five branches feed the shared summer. Frequency ranges, ±15 dB gain, continuously variable Q and outer shelving detents follow the 8200 manual. This models servo-stabilized output and hard bypass without interstage coupling or transformers. The output clamps approximate the ±24 V signal headroom of the ±28 V supply design. The published circuit concept and response determine the component values and Q processing. The 9202 amplifier sections are represented by linear op amps. The servo uses a TL071 proxy. Separate linear VCA halves reproduce the static center-tapped gain pot behavior. Exact 9202 noise, high-frequency distortion and balanced stereo output remain outside this reconstruction.",
  "groundNode": "0",
  "inputNode": "in",
  "layout": {
    "pots": [
      "Low Gain",
      "Low Frequency",
      "Low Q",
      "Low Mid Gain",
      "Low Mid Frequency",
      "Low Mid Q",
      "Mid Gain",
      "Mid Frequency",
      "Mid Q",
      "High Mid Gain",
      "High Mid Frequency",
      "High Mid Q",
      "High Gain",
      "High Frequency",
      "High Q"
    ],
    "switches": [
      "EQ Bypass",
      "Low Shelf",
      "High Shelf"
    ]
  },
  "name": "GML 8200 Series II Circuit Model",
  "outputNode": "out",
